{#ch08-0001}
# LOKASENNA

{#ch08-0002}
## {_Loki’s Wrangling_}

{#ch08-0003}
## Introductory Note

{#ch08-0004}
The {_Lokasenna_} is found only in {_Regius_}, where it follows the {_Hymiskvitha_}; Snorri quotes four lines of it, grouped together as a single stanza.

{#ch08-0005}
The poem is one of the most vigorous of the entire collection, and seems to have been preserved in exceptionally good condition. The exchange or contest of insults was dear to the Norse heart, and the {_Lokasenna_} consists chiefly of Loki’s taunts to the assembled gods and goddesses, and their largely ineffectual attempts to talk back to him. The author was evidently well versed in mythological lore, and the poem is full of references to incidents not elsewhere recorded. As to its date and origin there is the usual dispute, but the latter part of the tenth century and Iceland seem the best guesses.

{#ch08-0006}
The prose notes are long and of unusual interest. The introductory one links the poem closely to the {_Hymiskvitha_}, much as the {_Reginsmol_}, {_Fafnismol_} and {_Sigrdrifumol_} are linked together; the others fill in the narrative gaps in the dialogue—very like stage directions,—and provide a conclusion by relating Loki’s punishment, which, presumably, is here connected with the wrong incident. It is likely that often when the poem was recited during the two centuries or so before it was committed to writing, the speaker inserted some such explanatory comments, and the compiler of the collection followed this example by adding such explanations as he thought necessary. The {_Lokasenna_} is certainly much older than the {_Hymiskvitha_}, the connection between them being purely one of subject-matter; and the twelfth-century compiler evidently knew a good deal less about mythology than the author whose work he was annotating.

{#ch08-0007}
Ægir, who was also called Gymir, had prepared ale for the gods, after he had got the mighty kettle, as now has been told. To this feast came Othin and Frigg, his wife. Thor came not, as he was on a journey in the East. Sif, []{.pb n="152"}Thor’s wife, was there, and Bragi with Ithun, his wife. Tyr, who had but one hand, was there; the wolf Fenrir had bitten off his other hand when they had bound him. There were Njorth and Skathi his wife, Freyr and Freyja, and Vithar, the son of Othin. Loki was there, and Freyr’s []{.pb n="153"}servants Byggvir and Beyla. Many were there of the gods and elves.

{#ch08-0008}
Ægir had two serving-men, Fimafeng and Eldir. Glittering gold they had in place of firelight; the ale came in of itself; and great was the peace. The guests praised much the ability of Ægir’s serving-men. Loki might not endure that, and he slew Fimafeng. Then the gods shook their shields and howled at Loki and drove him away to the forest, and thereafter set to drinking again. Loki turned back, and outside he met Eldir. Loki spoke to him:

{#ch08-0009 ref="ls.1"}
::: stanza
“Speak now, Eldir, | for not one step
Farther shalt thou fare;
What ale-talk here | do they have within,
The sons of the glorious gods?”
:::

{#ch08-0010}
{_Eldir spake_}:

{#ch08-0011 ref="ls.2"}
::: stanza
“Of their weapons they talk, | and their might in war,
The sons of the glorious gods;
From the gods and elves | who are gathered here
No friend in words shalt thou find.”
:::

{#ch08-0012}
{_Loki spake_}:

{#ch08-0013 ref="ls.3"}
::: stanza
“In shall I go | into Ægir’s hall,
For the feast I fain would see;[]{.pb n="154"}
Bale and hatred | I bring to the gods,
And their mead with venom I mix.”
:::

{#ch08-0014}
{_Eldir spake_}:

{#ch08-0015 ref="ls.4"}
::: stanza
“If in thou goest | to Ægir’s hall,
And fain the feast wouldst see,
And with slander and spite | wouldst sprinkle the gods,
Think well lest they wipe it on thee.”
:::

{#ch08-0016}
{_Loki spake_}:

{#ch08-0017 ref="ls.5"}
::: stanza
“Bethink thee, Eldir, | if thou and I
Shall strive with spiteful speech;
Richer I grow | in ready words
If thou speakest too much to me.”
:::

{#ch08-0018}
Then Loki went into the hall, but when they who were there saw who had entered, they were all silent.

{#ch08-0019}
{_Loki spake_}:

{#ch08-0020 ref="ls.6"}
::: stanza
“Thirsty I come | into this thine hall,
I, Lopt, from a journey long,
To ask of the gods | that one should give
Fair mead for a drink to me.
:::

{#ch08-0021 ref="ls.7"}
::: stanza
“Why sit ye silent, | swollen with pride,
Ye gods, and no answer give?[]{.pb n="155"}
At your feast a place | and a seat prepare me,
Or bid me forth to fare.”
:::

{#ch08-0022}
{_Bragi spake_}:

{#ch08-0023 ref="ls.8"}
::: stanza
“A place and a seat | will the gods prepare
No more in their midst for thee;
For the gods know well | what men they wish
To find at their mighty feasts.”
:::

{#ch08-0024}
{_Loki spake_}:

{#ch08-0025 ref="ls.9"}
::: stanza
“Remember, Othin, | in olden days
That we both our blood have mixed;
Then didst thou promise | no ale to pour,
Unless it were brought for us both.”
:::

{#ch08-0026}
{_Othin spake_}:

{#ch08-0027 ref="ls.10"}
::: stanza
“Stand forth then, Vithar, | and let the wolf’s father
Find a seat at our feast;[]{.pb n="156"}
Lest evil should Loki | speak aloud
Here within Ægir’s hall.”
:::

{#ch08-0028}
Then Vithar arose and poured drink for Loki; but before he drank he spoke to the gods:

{#ch08-0029 ref="ls.11"}
::: stanza
“Hail to you, gods\! | ye goddesses, hail\!
Hail to the holy throng\!
Save for the god | who yonder sits,
Bragi there on the bench.”
:::

{#ch08-0030}
{_Bragi spake_}:

{#ch08-0031 ref="ls.12"}
::: stanza
“A horse and a sword | from my hoard will I give,
And a ring gives Bragi to boot,
That hatred thou makst not | among the gods;
So rouse not the great ones to wrath.”
:::

{#ch08-0032}
{_Loki spake_}:

{#ch08-0033 ref="ls.13"}
::: stanza
“In horses and rings | thou shalt never be rich,
Bragi, but both shalt thou lack;
Of the gods and elves | here together met
Least brave in battle art thou,
(And shyest thou art of the shot.)”
:::

{#ch08-0034}
{_Bragi spake_}:

{#ch08-0035 ref="ls.14"}
::: stanza
“Now were I without | as I am within,[]{.pb n="157"}
And here in Ægir’s hall,
Thine head would I bear | in mine hands away,
And pay thee the price of thy lies.”
:::

{#ch08-0036}
{_Loki spake_}:

{#ch08-0037 ref="ls.15"}
::: stanza
“In thy seat art thou bold, | not so are thy deeds,
Bragi, adorner of benches\!
Go out and fight | if angered thou feelest,
No hero such forethought has.”
:::

{#ch08-0038}
{_Ithun spake_}:

{#ch08-0039 ref="ls.16"}
::: stanza
“Well, prithee, Bragi, | his kinship weigh,
Since chosen as wish-son he was;
And speak not to Loki | such words of spite
Here within Ægir’s hall.”
:::

{#ch08-0040}
{_Loki spake_}:

{#ch08-0041 ref="ls.17"}
::: stanza
“Be silent, Ithun\! | thou art, I say,[]{.pb n="158"}
Of women most lustful in love,
Since thou thy washed-bright | arms didst wind
About thy brother’s slayer.”
:::

{#ch08-0042}
{_Ithun spake_}:

{#ch08-0043 ref="ls.18"}
::: stanza
“To Loki I speak not | with spiteful words
Here within Ægir’s hall;
And Bragi I calm, | who is hot with beer,
For I wish not that fierce they should fight.”
:::

{#ch08-0044}
{_Gefjun spake_}:

{#ch08-0045 ref="ls.19"}
::: stanza
“Why, ye gods twain, | with bitter tongues
Raise hate among us here?
Loki is famed | for his mockery foul,
And the dwellers in heaven he hates.”
:::

{#ch08-0046}
{_Loki spake_}:

{#ch08-0047 ref="ls.20"}
::: stanza
“Be silent, Gefjun\! | for now shall I say
Who led thee to evil life;
The boy so fair | gave a necklace bright,
And about him thy leg was laid.”
:::

{#ch08-0048}
[]{.pb n="159"}{_Othin spake_}:

{#ch08-0049 ref="ls.21"}
::: stanza
“Mad art thou, Loki, | and little of wit,
The wrath of Gefjun to rouse;
For the fate that is set | for all she sees,
Even as I, methinks.”
:::

{#ch08-0050}
{_Loki spake_}:

{#ch08-0051 ref="ls.22"}
::: stanza
“Be silent, Othin\! | not justly thou settest
The fate of the fight among men;
Oft gavst thou to him | who deserved not the gift,
To the baser, the battle’s prize.”
:::

{#ch08-0052}
{_Othin spake_}:

{#ch08-0053 ref="ls.23"}
::: stanza
“Though I gave to him | who deserved not the gift,
To the baser, the battle’s prize;
Winters eight | wast thou under the earth,
Milking the cows as a maid,
(Ay, and babes didst thou bear;
Unmanly thy soul must seem.)”
:::

{#ch08-0054}
[]{.pb n="160"}{_Loki spake_}:

{#ch08-0055 ref="ls.24"}
::: stanza
“They say that with spells | in Samsey once
Like witches with charms didst thou work;
And in witch’s guise | among men didst thou go;
Unmanly thy soul must seem.”
:::

{#ch08-0056}
{_Frigg spake_}:

{#ch08-0057 ref="ls.25"}
::: stanza
“Of the deeds ye two | of old have done
Ye should make no speech among men;
Whate’er ye have done | in days gone by,
Old tales should ne’er be told.”
:::

{#ch08-0058}
{_Loki spake_}:

{#ch08-0059 ref="ls.26"}
::: stanza
“Be silent, Frigg\! | thou art Fjorgyn’s wife,
But ever lustful in love;
For Vili and Ve, | thou wife of Vithrir,
Both in thy bosom have lain.”
:::

{#ch08-0060}
[]{.pb n="161"}{_Frigg spake_}:

{#ch08-0061 ref="ls.27"}
::: stanza
“If a son like Baldr | were by me now,
Here within Ægir’s hall,
From the sons of the gods | thou shouldst go not forth
Till thy fierceness in fight were tried.”
:::

{#ch08-0062}
{_Loki spake_}:

{#ch08-0063 ref="ls.28"}
::: stanza
“Thou wilt then, Frigg, | that further I tell
Of the ill that now I know;
Mine is the blame | that Baldr no more
Thou seest ride home to the hall.”
:::

{#ch08-0064}
{_Freyja spake_}:

{#ch08-0065 ref="ls.29"}
::: stanza
“Mad art thou, Loki, | that known thou makest
The wrong and shame thou hast wrought;
The fate of all | does Frigg know well,
Though herself she says it not.”
:::

{#ch08-0066}
{_Loki spake_}:

{#ch08-0067 ref="ls.30"}
::: stanza
“Be silent, Freyja\! | for fully I know thee,
Sinless thou art not thyself;[]{.pb n="162"}
Of the gods and elves | who are gathered here,
Each one as thy lover has lain.”
:::

{#ch08-0068}
{_Freyja spake_}:

{#ch08-0069 ref="ls.31"}
::: stanza
“False is thy tongue, | and soon shalt thou find
That it sings thee an evil song;
The gods are wroth, | and the goddesses all,
And in grief shalt thou homeward go.”
:::

{#ch08-0070}
{_Loki spake_}:

{#ch08-0071 ref="ls.32"}
::: stanza
“Be silent, Freyja\! | thou foulest witch,
And steeped full sore in sin;
In the arms of thy brother | the bright gods caught thee
When Freyja her wind set free.”
:::

{#ch08-0072}
{_Njorth spake_}:

{#ch08-0073 ref="ls.33"}
::: stanza
“Small ill does it work | though a woman may have
A lord or a lover or both;
But a wonder it is | that this womanish god
Comes hither, though babes he has borne.”
:::

{#ch08-0074}
[]{.pb n="163"}{_Loki spake_}:

{#ch08-0075 ref="ls.34"}
::: stanza
“Be silent, Njorth; | thou wast eastward sent,
To the gods as a hostage given;
And the daughters of Hymir | their privy had
When use did they make of thy mouth.”
:::

{#ch08-0076}
{_Njorth spake_}:

{#ch08-0077 ref="ls.35"}
::: stanza
“Great was my gain, | though long was I gone,
To the gods as a hostage given;
The son did I have | whom no man hates,
And foremost of gods is found.”
:::

{#ch08-0078}
{_Loki spake_}:

{#ch08-0079 ref="ls.36"}
::: stanza
“Give heed now, Njorth, | nor boast too high,
No longer I hold it hid;
With thy sister hadst thou | so fair a son,
Thus hadst thou no worse a hope.”
:::

{#ch08-0080}
{_Tyr spake_}:

{#ch08-0081 ref="ls.37"}
::: stanza
“Of the heroes brave | is Freyr the best
Here in the home of the gods;[]{.pb n="164"}
He harms not maids | nor the wives of men,
And the bound from their fetters he frees.”
:::

{#ch08-0082}
{_Loki spake_}:

{#ch08-0083 ref="ls.38"}
::: stanza
“Be silent, Tyr\! | for between two men
Friendship thou ne’er couldst fashion;
Fain would I tell | how Fenrir once
Thy right hand rent from thee.”
:::

{#ch08-0084}
{_Tyr spake_}:

{#ch08-0085 ref="ls.39"}
::: stanza
“My hand do I lack, | but Hrothvitnir thou,
And the loss brings longing to both;
Ill fares the wolf | who shall ever await
In fetters the fall of the gods.”
:::

{#ch08-0086}
{_Loki spake_}:

{#ch08-0087 ref="ls.40"}
::: stanza
“Be silent, Tyr\! | for a son with me
Thy wife once chanced to win;
Not a penny, methinks, | wast thou paid for the wrong,
Nor wast righted an inch, poor wretch.”
:::

{#ch08-0088}
{_Freyr spake_}:

{#ch08-0089 ref="ls.41"}
::: stanza
“By the mouth of the river | the wolf remains[]{.pb n="165"}
Till the gods to destruction go;
Thou too shalt soon, | if thy tongue is not stilled,
Be fettered, thou forger of ill.”
:::

{#ch08-0090}
{_Loki spake_}:

{#ch08-0091 ref="ls.42"}
::: stanza
“The daughter of Gymir | with gold didst thou buy,
And sold thy sword to boot;
But when Muspell’s sons | through Myrkwood ride,
Thou shalt weaponless wait, poor wretch.”
:::

{#ch08-0092}
{_Byggvir spake_}:

{#ch08-0093 ref="ls.43"}
::: stanza
“Had I birth so famous | as Ingunar-Freyr,
And sat in so lofty a seat,[]{.pb n="166"}
I would crush to marrow | this croaker of ill,
And beat all his body to bits.”
:::

{#ch08-0094}
{_Loki spake_}:

{#ch08-0095 ref="ls.44"}
::: stanza
“What little creature | goes crawling there,
Snuffling and snapping about?
At Freyr’s ears ever | wilt thou be found,
Or muttering hard at the mill.”
:::

{#ch08-0096}
{_Byggvir spake_}:

{#ch08-0097 ref="ls.45"}
::: stanza
“Byggvir my name, | and nimble am I,
As gods and men do grant;
And here am I proud | that the children of Hropt
Together all drink ale.”
:::

{#ch08-0098}
{_Loki spake_}:

{#ch08-0099 ref="ls.46"}
::: stanza
“Be silent, Byggvir\! | thou never couldst set
Their shares of the meat for men;
Hid in straw on the floor, | they found thee not
When heroes were fain to fight.”
:::

{#ch08-0100}
{_Heimdall spake_}:

{#ch08-0101 ref="ls.47"}
::: stanza
“Drunk art thou, Loki, | and mad are thy deeds,
Why, Loki, leavst thou this not?[]{.pb n="167"}
For drink beyond measure | will lead all men
No thought of their tongues to take.”
:::

{#ch08-0102}
{_Loki spake_}:

{#ch08-0103 ref="ls.48"}
::: stanza
“Be silent, Heimdall\! | in days long since
Was an evil fate for thee fixed;
With back held stiff | must thou ever stand,
As warder of heaven to watch.”
:::

{#ch08-0104}
{_Skathi spake_}:

{#ch08-0105 ref="ls.49"}
::: stanza
“Light art thou, Loki, | but longer thou mayst not
In freedom flourish thy tail;
On the rocks the gods bind thee | with bowels torn
Forth from thy frost-cold son.”
:::

{#ch08-0106}
{_Loki spake_}:

{#ch08-0107 ref="ls.50"}
::: stanza
“Though on rocks the gods bind me | with bowels torn
Forth from my frost-cold son,[]{.pb n="168"}
I was first and last | at the deadly fight
There where Thjazi we caught.”
:::

{#ch08-0108}
{_Skathi spake_}:

{#ch08-0109 ref="ls.51"}
::: stanza
“Wert thou first and last | at the deadly fight
There where Thjazi was caught,
From my dwellings and fields | shall ever come forth
A counsel cold for thee.”
:::

{#ch08-0110}
{_Loki spake_}:

{#ch08-0111 ref="ls.52"}
::: stanza
“More lightly thou spakest | with Laufey’s son,
When thou badst me come to thy bed;
Such things must be known | if now we two
Shall seek our sins to tell.”
:::

{#ch08-0112}
Then Sif came forward and poured mead for Loki in a crystal cup, and said:

{#ch08-0113 ref="ls.53"}
::: stanza
“Hail to thee, Loki, | and take thou here
The crystal cup of old mead;
For me at least, | alone of the gods,
Blameless thou knowest to be.”
:::

{#ch08-0114}
[]{.pb n="169"}He took the horn, and drank therefrom:

{#ch08-0115 ref="ls.54"}
::: stanza
“Alone thou wert | if truly thou wouldst
All men so shyly shun;
But one do I know | full well, methinks,
Who had thee from Hlorrithi’s arms,—
(Loki the crafty in lies.)”
:::

{#ch08-0116}
{_Beyla spake_}:

{#ch08-0117 ref="ls.55"}
::: stanza
“The mountains shake, | and surely I think
From his home comes Hlorrithi now;
He will silence the man | who is slandering here
Together both gods and men.”
:::

{#ch08-0118}
{_Loki spake_}:

{#ch08-0119 ref="ls.56"}
::: stanza
“Be silent, Beyla\! | thou art Byggvir’s wife,
And deep art thou steeped in sin;
A greater shame | to the gods came ne’er,
Befouled thou art with thy filth.”
:::

{#ch08-0120}
Then came Thor forth, and spake:

{#ch08-0121 ref="ls.57"}
::: stanza
“Unmanly one, cease, | or the mighty hammer,
Mjollnir, shall close thy mouth;[]{.pb n="170"}
Thy shoulder-cliff | shall I cleave from thy neck,
And so shall thy life be lost.”
:::

{#ch08-0122}
{_Loki spake_}:

{#ch08-0123 ref="ls.58"}
::: stanza
“Lo, in has come | the son of Earth:
Why threaten so loudly, Thor?
Less fierce thou shalt go | to fight with the wolf
When he swallows Sigfather up.”
:::

{#ch08-0124}
{_Thor spake_}:

{#ch08-0125 ref="ls.59"}
::: stanza
“Unmanly one, cease, | or the mighty hammer,
Mjollnir, shall close thy mouth;
I shall hurl thee up | and out in the East,
Where men shall see thee no more.”
:::

{#ch08-0126}
{_Loki spake_}:

{#ch08-0127 ref="ls.60"}
::: stanza
“That thou hast fared | on the East-road forth
To men shouldst thou say no more;[]{.pb n="171"}
In the thumb of a glove | didst thou hide, thou great one,
And there forgot thou wast Thor.”
:::

{#ch08-0128}
{_Thor spake_}:

{#ch08-0129 ref="ls.61"}
::: stanza
“Unmanly one, cease, | or the mighty hammer,
Mjollnir, shall close thy mouth;
My right hand shall smite thee | with Hrungnir’s slayer,
Till all thy bones are broken.”
:::

{#ch08-0130}
{_Loki spake_}:

{#ch08-0131 ref="ls.62"}
::: stanza
“A long time still | do I think to live,
Though thou threatenest thus with thy hammer;
Rough seemed the straps | of Skrymir’s wallet,
When thy meat thou mightest not get,
(And faint from hunger didst feel.)”
:::

{#ch08-0132}
{_Thor spake_}:

{#ch08-0133 ref="ls.63"}
::: stanza
“Unmanly one, cease, | or the mighty hammer,
Mjollnir, shall close thy mouth;[]{.pb n="172"}
The slayer of Hrungnir | shall send thee to hell,
And down to the gate of death.”
:::

{#ch08-0134}
{_Loki spake_}:

{#ch08-0135 ref="ls.64"}
::: stanza
“I have said to the gods | and the sons of the gods
The things that whetted my thoughts;
But before thee alone | do I now go forth,
For thou fightest well, I ween.
:::

{#ch08-0136 ref="ls.65"}
::: stanza
“Ale hast thou brewed, | but, Ægir, now
Such feasts shalt thou make no more;
O’er all that thou hast | which is here within
Shall play the flickering flames,
(And thy back shall be burnt with fire.)”
:::

{#ch08-0137}
And after that Loki hid himself in Franang’s waterfall in the guise of a salmon, and there the gods took him. He was bound with the bowels of his son Vali, but his son Narfi was changed to a wolf. Skathi took a poison-snake and fastened it up over Loki’s face, and the poison dropped thereon. Sigyn, Loki’s wife, sat there and held a shell under the poison, but when the shell was full she bore away the poison, and meanwhile the poison dropped on Loki. Then he struggled so hard that the whole earth shook therewith; and now that is called an earthquake.[]{.pb n="151"}

{#ch08-0138 on="#ch08-0007"}
::::: commentary
[]{.pb n="152"}{_Ægir_}: the sea-god; Snorri gives Hler as another of his names, but he is not elsewhere called Gymir, which is the name of the giant, Gerth’s father, in the {_Skirnismol_}. On Ægir cf. {_Grimnismol_}, 45, and {_Hymiskvitha_}, 1. {_Frigg_}: though Othin’s wife is often mentioned, she plays only a minor part in the Eddic poems; cf. {_Voluspo_}, 34, {_Vafthruthnismol_}, 1, and {_Grimnismol_}, introductory prose. {_Thor_}: the compiler is apparently a trifle confused as to Thor’s movements; the “journey in the East” here mentioned cannot be the one described in the {_Hymiskvitha_}, nor yet the one narrated by Snorri, as Loki was with Thor throughout that expedition. He probably means no more than that Thor was off killing giants. {_Sif_}: concerning Thor’s wife the chief incident is that Loki cut off her hair, and, at the command of the wrathful Thor, was compelled to have the dwarfs fashion her a new supply of hair out of gold; cf. {_Harbarthsljoth_}, 48. {_Bragi_}: the god of poetry; cf. {_Grimnismol_}, 44 and note. {_Ithun_}: the goddess of youth; cf. note on {_Skirnismol_}, 19. Ithun is not mentioned by name in any other of the Eddic poems, but Snorri tells in detail how the giant Thjazi stole her and her apples, explaining the reference in {_Harbarthsljoth_}, 19 (q. v.). {_Tyr_}: the god of battle; cf. {_Hymiskvitha_}, 4, and (concerning his dealings with the wolf Fenrir) {_Voluspo_}, 39, note. {_Njorth_}: the chief of the Wanes, and father of {_Freyr_} and {_Freyja_}; cf. (concerning the whole family) {_Skirnismol_}, introductory prose and note, also {_Voluspo_}, 21 and note. {_Skathi_}: Njorth’s wife was the daughter of the giant Thjazi; cf. {_Harbarthsljoth_}, 19, note, and {_Grimnismol_}, 11. {_Vithar_}: the silent god, the son of Othin who avenged his father by slaying the wolf Fenrir; cf. {_Voluspo_}, 54, {_Vafthruthnismol_}, 51, and {_Grimnismol_}, 17. {_Loki_}: the mischief-making fire-god; in addition to the many references to his career in the {_Lokasenna_}, cf. particularly {_Voluspo_}, 32 and 35, and notes. {_Byggvir_} and {_Beyla_}: not mentioned elsewhere in the poems; Freyr’s conspicuous servant is Skirnir, hero of the {_Skirnismol_}. {_Fimafeng_} (“The Swift Handler”) []{.pb n="153"}and {_Eldir_} (“The Man of the Fire”): mentioned only in connection with this incident. {_Glittering gold_}: Ægir’s use of gold to light his hall, which was often thought of as under the sea, was responsible for the phrase “flame of the flood,” and sundry kindred phrases, meaning “gold.”[]{.pb n="154"}
:::::

{#ch08-0139 on="ls.6"}
::::: commentary
{_Lopt_}: like Lothur (cf. {_Voluspo_}, 18) another name for Loki; cf. {_Hyndluljoth_}, 43, and Svipdagsmol, 42.
:::::

{#ch08-0140 on="ls.7"}
::::: commentary
In the manuscript this stanza begins with a small letter, and Heinzel unites it with stanza 6.[]{.pb n="155"}
:::::

{#ch08-0141 on="ls.8"}
::::: commentary
{_Bragi_}: cf. note on introductory prose. Why Loki taunts him with cowardice (stanzas 11–13–15) is not clear, for poetry, of which Bragi was the patron, was generally associated in the Norse mind with peculiar valor, and most of the skaldic poets were likewise noted fighters.
:::::

{#ch08-0142 on="ls.9"}
::::: commentary
There exists no account of any incident in which Othin and Loki thus swore blood-brotherhood, but they were so often allied in enterprises that the idea is wholly reasonable. The common process of “mingling blood” was carried out quite literally, and the promise of which Loki speaks is characteristic of those which, in the sagas, often accompanied the ceremony; cf. {_Brot af Sigurtharkvithu_}, 18 and note.
:::::

{#ch08-0143 on="ls.10"}
::::: commentary
In stanzas 10–31 the manuscript has nothing to indicate the identity of the several speakers, but these are uniformly clear []{.pb n="156"}enough through the context. {_Vithar_}: cf. note on introductory prose. {_The wolf’s father_}: Loki; cf. {_Voluspo_}, 39 and note.
:::::

{#ch08-0144 on="ls.13"}
::::: commentary
Sijmons makes one line of lines 4–5 by cutting out a part of each; Finnur Jonsson rejects 5 as spurious.
:::::

{#ch08-0145 on="ls.14"}
::::: commentary
The text of line 4 is somewhat obscure, and has been []{.pb n="157"}variously emended, one often adopted suggestion making the line read, “Little is that for thy lies.”
:::::

{#ch08-0146 on="ls.15"}
::::: commentary
{_Adorner of benches_}: this epithet presumably implies that Bragi is not only slothful, but also effeminate, for a very similar word, “pride of the benches,” means a bride.
:::::

{#ch08-0147 on="ls.16"}
::::: commentary
{_Ithun_}: Bragi’s wife; cf. note on introductory prose. The goddesses who, finding that their husbands are getting the worst of it, take up the cudgels with Loki, all find themselves confronted with undeniable facts in their own careers; cf. stanzas 26 (Frigg), 52 (Skathi) and 54 (Sif). Gefjun and Freyja are silenced in similar fashion. {_Wish-son_}: adopted son; Loki was the son of the giant Farbauti and the giantess Laufey, and hence was not of the race of the gods, but had been virtually adopted by Othin, who subsequently had good reason to regret it.[]{.pb n="158"}
:::::

{#ch08-0148 on="ls.17"}
::::: commentary
We do not even know who Ithun’s brother was, much less who slew him.
:::::

{#ch08-0149 on="ls.19"}
::::: commentary
{_Gefjun_}: a goddess, not elsewhere mentioned in the poems, who, according to Snorri, was served by the women who died maidens. Beyond this nothing is known of her. Lines 3–4 in the manuscript are puzzling, and have been freely emended.
:::::

{#ch08-0150 on="ls.20"}
::::: commentary
Nothing is known of the incident here mentioned. There is a good deal of confusion as to various of the gods and goddesses, and it has been suggested that Gefjun is really Frigg under another name, with a little of Freyja—whose attributes were frequently confused with Frigg’s—thrown in. Certainly Othin’s []{.pb n="159"}answer (stanza 21, lines 3–4) fits Frigg perfectly, for she shared his knowledge of the future, whereas it has no relation to anything known of Gefjun. As for the necklace (line 3), it may be the Brisings’ necklace, which appears in the {_Thrymskvitha_} as Freyja’s, but which, in some mythological writings, is assigned to Frigg.
:::::

{#ch08-0151 on="ls.21"}
::::: commentary
Snorri quotes line 1; cf. note on stanza 29.
:::::

{#ch08-0152 on="ls.23"}
::::: commentary
There is no other reference to Loki’s having spent eight years underground, or to his cow-milking. On one occasion, however, he did bear offspring. A giant had undertaken to build the gods a fortress, his reward being Freyja and the sun and moon, provided the work was done by a given time. His sole helper was his horse, Svathilfari. The work being nearly done, and the gods fearing to lose Freyja and the sun and moon, Loki []{.pb n="160"}turned himself into a mare, and so effectually distracted Svathilfari from his task that shortly afterwards Loki gave birth to Othin’s eight-legged horse, Sleipnir. In such contests of abuse a man was not infrequently taunted with having borne children; cf. {_Helgakvitha Hundingsbana_} I, 39–45. One or two of the last three lines may be spurious.
:::::

{#ch08-0153 on="ls.24"}
::::: commentary
{_Samsey_}: perhaps the Danish island of Samsö. Othin was the god of magic, but there is no other reference to his ever having disguised himself as a witch.
:::::

{#ch08-0154 on="ls.25"}
::::: commentary
{_Frigg_}: Othin’s wife; cf. note to introductory prose.
:::::

{#ch08-0155 on="ls.26"}
::::: commentary
{_Fjorgyn_}: Othin; cf. {_Voluspo_}, 56 and note. {_Vili_} and {_Ve_}: Othin’s brothers, who appear merely as, with Othin, the sons of Bur and Bestla; cf. {_Voluspo_}, 4. The {_Ynglingasaga_} says that, during one of Othin’s protracted absences, his two brothers took Frigg as their mistress. {_Vithrir_}: another name for Othin.[]{.pb n="161"}
:::::

{#ch08-0156 on="ls.27"}
::::: commentary
On the death of Baldr, slain through Loki’s cunning by the blind Hoth, cf. {_Voluspo_}, 32 and note.
:::::

{#ch08-0157 on="ls.29"}
::::: commentary
{_Freyja_}: daughter of Njorth and sister of Freyr; cf. note on introductory prose. Snorri, in speaking of Frigg’s knowledge of the future, makes a stanza out of {_Lokasenna_}, 21, 1; 47, 2; 29, 3–4, thus: “Mad art thou, Loki,   \|   and little of wit, / Why, Loki, leavst thou this not? / The fate of all   \|   does Frigg know well, / Though herself she says it not.”
:::::

{#ch08-0158 on="ls.30"}
::::: commentary
According to Snorri, Freyja was a model of fidelity to her husband, Oth.[]{.pb n="162"}
:::::

{#ch08-0159 on="ls.32"}
::::: commentary
Before each of stanzas 32–42 the manuscript indicates the speaker, through the initial letter of the name written in the margin. {_Thy brother_}: Freyr; there is no other indication that such a relation existed between these two, but they themselves were the product of such a union; cf. stanza 36 and note.
:::::

{#ch08-0160 on="ls.33"}
::::: commentary
{_Njorth_}: father of Freyr and Freyja, and given by the Wanes as a hostage, in exchange for Hönir, at the close of the first war; cf. {_Voluspo_}, 21 and note, also {_Skirnismol_}, introductory prose and note. {_Babes_}: cf. stanza 23 and note. Bugge suggests that this clause may have been a late insertion.[]{.pb n="163"}
:::::

{#ch08-0161 on="ls.34"}
::::: commentary
{_Daughters of Hymir_}: we have no clue to who these were, though Hymir is doubtless the frost-giant of the {_Hymiskvitha_} (q.v.). Loki’s point is that Njorth is not a god, but the product of an inferior race (the Wanes).
:::::

{#ch08-0162 on="ls.35"}
::::: commentary
{_The son_}: Freyr.
:::::

{#ch08-0163 on="ls.36"}
::::: commentary
{_Thy sister_}: the {_Ynglingasaga_} supports this story of Njorth’s having had two children by his sister before he came among the gods. Snorri, on the other hand, specifically says that Freyr and Freyja were born after Njorth came to the gods.
:::::

{#ch08-0164 on="ls.37"}
::::: commentary
{_Tyr_}: the god of battle; cf. notes on {_Hymiskvitha_}, 4, and {_Voluspo_}, 39. {_Freyr_}; concerning his noble qualities cf. {_Skirnismol_}, introductory prose and note.[]{.pb n="164"}
:::::

{#ch08-0165 on="ls.38"}
::::: commentary
Snorri mentions Tyr’s incompetence as a peacemaker. {_Fenrir_}: the wolf, Loki’s son; cf. {_Voluspo_}, 39.
:::::

{#ch08-0166 on="ls.39"}
::::: commentary
{_Hrothvitnir_} (“The Mighty Wolf”): Fenrir, who awaits in chains the final battle and death at the hands of Vithar. The manuscript has a metrical error in line 3, which has led to various emendations, all with much the same meaning.
:::::

{#ch08-0167 on="ls.40"}
::::: commentary
{_Thy wife_}: there is no other reference to Tyr’s wife, nor do we know who was the son in question.[]{.pb n="165"}
:::::

{#ch08-0168 on="ls.41"}
::::: commentary
{_The mouth of the river_}: according to Snorri, the chained Fenrir “roars horribly, and the slaver runs from his mouth, and makes the river called Vam; he lies there till the doom of the gods.” Freyr’s threat is actually carried out; cf. concluding prose.
:::::

{#ch08-0169 on="ls.42"}
::::: commentary
{_The daughter of Gymir_}: Gerth, heroine of the {_Skirnismol_}, which gives the details of Freyr’s loss of his sword. {_Muspell’s sons_}: the name Muspell is not used elsewhere in the poems; Snorri uses it frequently, but only in this same phrase, “Muspell’s sons.” They are the dwellers in the fire-world, Muspellsheim, led by Surt against the gods in the last battle; cf. {_Voluspo_}, 47 and 52 and notes. {_Myrkwood_}: here the dark forest bounding the fire-world; in the {_Atlakvitha_} (stanza 3) the name is used of another boundary forest.
:::::

{#ch08-0170 on="ls.43"}
::::: commentary
{_Byggvir_}: one of Freyr’s two servants; cf. introductory prose. {_Ingunar-Freyr_}: the name is not used elsewhere in the poems, or by Snorri; it may be the genitive of a woman’s name, Ingun, the unknown sister of Njorth who was Freyr’s mother (cf. stanza 36), or a corruption of the name Ingw, used for Freyr (Fro) in old German mythology.[]{.pb n="166"}
:::::

{#ch08-0171 on="ls.44"}
::::: commentary
Beginning with this stanza, the names of the speakers are lacking in the manuscript. {_The mill_}: i.e., at slaves’ tasks.
:::::

{#ch08-0172 on="ls.45"}
::::: commentary
Nothing further is known of either Byggvir’s swiftness or his cowardice. {_Hropt_}: Othin.
:::::

{#ch08-0173 on="ls.47"}
::::: commentary
{_Heimdall_}: besides being the watchman of the gods (cf. {_Voluspo_}, 27), he appears also as the god of light (cf. {_Thrymskvitha_}, 14), and possibly also as a complex cultural deity in the []{.pb n="167"}{_Rigsthula_}. He was a son of Othin, born of nine sisters; cf. {_Hyndluljoth_}, 37–40. In the last battle he and Loki slay one another. Line 2 is quoted by Snorri; cf. stanza 29, note.
:::::

{#ch08-0174 on="ls.49"}
::::: commentary
{_Skathi_}: the wife of Njorth, and daughter of the giant Thjazi, concerning whose death cf. {_Harbarthsljoth_}, 19, note. {_Bowels_}, etc.: according to the prose note at the end of the {_Lokasenna_}, the gods bound Loki with the bowels of his son Vali, and changed his other son, Narfi, into a wolf. Snorri turns the story about, Vali being the wolf, who tears his brother to pieces, the gods then using Narfi’s intestines to bind Loki. Narfi—and presumably Vali—were the sons of Loki and his wife, Sigyn. They appear only in this episode, though Narfi (or Nari) is named by Snorri in his list of Loki’s children. Cf. concluding prose, and note.[]{.pb n="168"}
:::::

{#ch08-0175 on="ls.52"}
::::: commentary
{_Laufey’s son_}: Loki; not much is known of his parents beyond their names. His father was the giant Farbauti, his mother Laufey, sometimes called Nal. There is an elaborate but far-fetched hypothesis explaining these three on the basis of a nature-myth. There is no other reference to such a relation between Skathi and Loki as he here suggests.
:::::

{#ch08-0176 on="ls.53"}
::::: commentary
{_Sif_}: Thor’s wife; cf. {_Harbarthsljoth_}, 48, where her infidelity is again mentioned. The manuscript omits the proper name []{.pb n="169"}from the preceding prose, and a few editors have, obviously in error, attributed the speech to Beyla.
:::::

{#ch08-0177 on="ls.54"}
::::: commentary
{_Hlorrithi_}: Thor. Line 5 is probably spurious.
:::::

{#ch08-0178 on="ls.55"}
::::: commentary
{_Beyla_}: Freyr’s servant, wife of Byggvir; cf. introductory prose and note.
:::::

{#ch08-0179 on="ls.57"}
::::: commentary
{_Mjollnir_}: concerning Thor’s famous hammer see particularly {_Thrymskvitha_}, 1 and note. {_Shoulder-cliff_}: head; concerning []{.pb n="170"}the use of such diction in the {_Edda_}, cf. introductory note to {_Hymiskvitha_}. The manuscript indicates line 3 as the beginning of a stanza, but this is apparently a scribal error.
:::::

{#ch08-0180 on="ls.58"}
::::: commentary
{_Son of Earth_}: Thor, son of Othin and Jorth (Earth). The manuscript omits the word “son,” but all editors have agreed in supplying it. {_The wolf_}: Fenrir, Loki’s son, who slays Othin ({_Sigfather_}: “Father of Victory”) in the final battle. Thor, according to Snorri and to the {_Voluspo_}, 56, fights with Mithgarthsorm and not with Fenrir, who is killed by Vithar.
:::::

{#ch08-0181 on="ls.59"}
::::: commentary
Lines 1–2 are abbreviated in the manuscript, as also in stanzas 61 and 63.
:::::

{#ch08-0182 on="ls.60"}
::::: commentary
Loki’s taunt that Thor hid in the thumb of Skrymir’s glove is similar to that of Othin, {_Harbarthsljoth_}, 26, in the note to which the story is outlined. Line 4 is identical with line 3 of {_Harbarthsljoth_}, 26.[]{.pb n="171"}
:::::

{#ch08-0183 on="ls.61"}
::::: commentary
{_Hrungnir’s slayer_}: the hammer; the story of how Thor slew this stone-headed giant is indicated in {_Harbarthsljoth_}, 14–15, and outlined in the note to stanza 14 of that poem.
:::::

{#ch08-0184 on="ls.62"}
::::: commentary
On the day following the adventure of the glove, Thor, Loki and Thor’s servants proceed on their way in company with Skrymir, who puts all their food in his wallet. At evening Skrymir goes to sleep, and Thor tries to get at the food, but cannot loosen the straps of the wallet. In a rage he smites Skrymir three times on the head with his hammer, but the giant—who, it subsequently appears, deftly dodges the blows—is totally undisturbed. Line 5 may well be spurious.[]{.pb n="172"}
:::::

{#ch08-0185 on="ls.65"}
::::: commentary
{_The flames_}: the fire that consumes the world on the last day; cf. {_Voluspo_}, 57. Line 5 may be spurious.
:::::

{#ch08-0186 on="#ch08-0137"}
::::: commentary
\: Snorri tells the same story, with minor differences, but makes it the consequence of Loki’s part in the slaying of Baldr, which undoubtedly represents the correct tradition. The compiler of the poems either was confused or thought the incident was []{.pb n="173"}useful as indicating what finally happened to Loki. Possibly he did not mean to imply that Loki’s fate was brought upon him by his abuse of the gods, but simply tried to round out the story. {_Franang_}: “Gleaming Water.” {_Vali_} and {_Narfi_}: cf. stanza 49 and note. {_Sigyn_}: cf. {_Voluspo_}, 35, the only other place where she is mentioned in the poems. Snorri omits the naive note about earthquakes, his narrative ending with the words, “And there he lies till the destruction of the gods.”[]{.pb n="174"}
:::::

